        modelAnswer: 'Classical Psychoanalysis, developed by Austrian neurologist Sigmund Freud in the late 19th century, represents the foundational paradigm of modern psychotherapy. Evolving from his work with Josef Breuer on hysteria (*Studies on Hysteria*, 1895), Freud demonstrated that psychological symptoms result from repressed traumatic memories and unconscious conflict, which can be resolved through the "talking cure" and catharsis.\n\nFreud conceptualized the human mind through two complementary frameworks: the Topographical Model (Conscious, Preconscious, and Unconscious) and the Structural Model. The structural model posits three competing agencies: (1) The Id, operating on the Pleasure Principle, driven by innate instinctual libido and aggressive impulses (Thanatos); (2) The Ego, operating on the Reality Principle, serving as the executive mediator between the Id\'s urges, external constraints, and moral demands through defense mechanisms; and (3) The Superego, representing internalized societal values and moral conscience.\n\nTo bypass ego defenses and access repressed material, classical psychoanalysis employs five core techniques:\n1. Free Association: The patient lies on a couch and adheres to the "fundamental rule" of verbalizing every thought without censorship.\n2. Dream Analysis: Deemed the "royal road to the unconscious" (*The Interpretation of Dreams*, 1900), the analyst decodes the superficial Manifest Content to uncover the disguised Latent Content.\n3. Analysis of Resistance: Examining unconscious defensiveness (lateness, silences) designed to ward off painful insight.\n4. Analysis of Transference: The central engine of therapeutic change, wherein the patient unconsciously projects past feelings, conflicts, and expectations regarding early caregivers onto the analyst.\n5. Interpretation and Working Through: Facilitating cognitive insight followed by emotional catharsis and behavioral integration.\n\nWhile classical psychoanalysis faced criticism for prolonged duration and non-falsifiability, contemporary Short-Term Psychodynamic Psychotherapy (STPP) retains these core insights within an empirically validated clinical framework.',
