        modelAnswer: 'Behaviour Therapy represents an empirical, action-oriented system of psychotherapy grounded in the scientific laws of classical conditioning (Pavlov, Watson), operant learning (Skinner), and observational modelling (Bandura). Unlike psychoanalysis, behaviour therapy posits that abnormal behaviours are learned maladaptive habits that can be unlearned through systematic conditioning techniques.\n\nIn 1958, Joseph Wolpe formulated Systematic Desensitization (*Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition*), the cornerstone behavioural therapy for phobias. Wolpe\'s principle of Reciprocal Inhibition states that if a response incompatible with anxiety (such as deep muscle relaxation) can be made to occur simultaneously with anxiety-provoking stimuli, the conditioned bond between the stimuli and anxiety will be severed. The protocol involves three steps: (1) training in Progressive Muscle Relaxation; (2) constructing an Anxiety Hierarchy ranging from least to most feared scenarios (rated 0–100 on the Subjective Units of Distress / SUDS scale); and (3) graded desensitization, where the client pairs relaxation with each hierarchy step until no anxiety is evoked.\n\nContrasting with gradual desensitization, Flooding involves prolonged, continuous in vivo exposure to high-intensity feared stimuli without the opportunity for avoidance. Because sympathetic autonomic arousal cannot be physiologically maintained indefinitely, prolonged exposure produces natural habituation and extinction. Implosion therapy represents the imaginal variant of flooding, confronting exaggerated psychodynamic imagery.\n\nAdditional modalities include Aversion Therapy (pairing undesirable habits with noxious stimuli like emetics, constrained today by strict ethical limits), Observational Modelling (Bandura, 1977), and Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP; Foa & Kozak, 1986), which remains the empirical gold standard for dismantling compulsive rituals in OCD.',
