        modelAnswer: 'A Token Economy represents a comprehensive, contingency-based behaviour modification system grounded in B.F. Skinner\'s principles of operant conditioning. Formulated by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan H. Azrin in 1968 (*The Token Economy: A Motivational System for Therapy and Rehabilitation*), the system was developed to overcome severe avolition, apathy, and functional deterioration in institutionalized psychiatric populations.\n\nA token economy functions through the systematic coordination of five structural elements:\n1. Target Behaviours: Specific, observable, and measurable adaptive behaviours (such as personal hygiene, bed-making, vocational participation, and social cooperation) that are explicitly defined.\n2. Tokens: Conditioned secondary reinforcers (points, chips, cards) that possess no intrinsic value but acquire reinforcing power through pairing with primary reinforcers. Tokens are dispensed immediately following the performance of a target behaviour, bridging the delay of gratification.\n3. Back-Up Reinforcers: High-value commodities, activities, or privileges (snacks, leisure passes, television time) that clients can purchase using their earned tokens.\n4. Exchange Rates: Transparent, publicly posted schedules establishing the number of tokens earned per behaviour and the token cost of each back-up reinforcer.\n5. Response Cost: A pre-determined deduction of tokens for specific disruptive or violent actions.\n\nToken economies have proven highly effective across psychiatric rehabilitation wards, special education classrooms, and addiction treatment (voucher-based contingency management for abstinence; Higgins). However, two major challenges require clinical attention: (1) The Generalization Deficit, wherein reinforced behaviours extinguish once the individual enters the natural community, necessitating structured "fading" into natural social praise and internal pride; and (2) Ethical Protections, codified in legislation such as India\'s Mental Healthcare Act 2017, establishing that basic human rights—including food, sleep, shelter, and family contact—can never be made contingent on token earnings.',
