        modelAnswer: 'Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (CBT) is an active, structured, time-limited, and empirically validated psychotherapy that posits that emotional and behavioural disturbances are maintained by distorted cognitive processing and maladaptive core schemas.\n\nCBT developed from two foundational clinical traditions: Albert Ellis\'s Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT; 1962) and Aaron T. Beck\'s Cognitive Therapy (1976, 1979). Ellis established the ABCDE model, demonstrating that Activating events (A) do not directly generate emotional Consequences (C); rather, consequences are mediated by the individual\'s Belief system (B). Irrational, dogmatic beliefs ("I must be approved by everyone") produce disturbance, which is resolved through Disputing (D) to achieve an Effective new philosophy (E). Beck formulated the cognitive model of psychopathology, organizing human cognition into a three-tiered hierarchy: (1) Core Schemas (deep, pervasive beliefs regarding Self, Others, and World); (2) Intermediate Beliefs (conditional assumptions, rules, and attitudes); and (3) Automatic Negative Thoughts (ANTs; rapid, situation-specific appraisals).\n\nWhen schemas are triggered by stress, they distort information processing through characteristic cognitive distortions: All-or-Nothing thinking (dichotomous reasoning), Catastrophizing (anticipating disaster), Mind Reading, Selective Abstraction (filtering out positive data), and Emotional Reasoning ("I feel guilty, therefore I am bad").\n\nTherapeutically, CBT operates on Collaborative Empiricism: the clinician and client act as scientific co-investigators testing thoughts as empirical hypotheses. Core clinical techniques include: Socratic Questioning (guided discovery), multi-column Thought Records, Behavioural Activation (scheduling mastery and pleasure activities), and Behavioural Experiments to test catastrophic predictions in real life. Supported by thousands of clinical trials, CBT represents the premier first-line treatment for depression, anxiety, OCD, and stress disorders.',
