        modelAnswer: 'Group Psychotherapy represents a specialized psychological modality where a trained clinician treats a small cohort of participants simultaneously, utilizing group dynamics and interpersonal interactions to achieve psychological transformation. The preeminent theorist of group psychotherapy is Irvin D. Yalom (*The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy*, 2020), who demonstrated that an interactive therapy group acts as a "Social Microcosm"—an authentic miniature reflection of the members\' social universes, where individual relational styles, maladaptive defenses, and attachment patterns naturally emerge in real time.\n\nYalom identified eleven primary Therapeutic (Curative) Factors that facilitate healing:\n1. Universality: The profound realization that one is not alone or uniquely defective in one\'s psychological suffering, dispelling toxic alienation.\n2. Installation of Hope: Observing positive change in fellow members generates optimism.\n3. Imparting Information: Didactic instruction and peer guidance.\n4. Altruism: Experiencing self-worth through offering genuine help to others.\n5. The Corrective Recapitulation of the Primary Family Group: Safely working through unresolved sibling and parental dynamics.\n6. Development of Socializing Techniques: Acquiring interpersonal communication skills through peer feedback.\n7. Imitative Behaviour: Modelling healthy coping styles observed in peers and the facilitator.\n8. Interpersonal Learning: The core curative engine, where members receive honest, supportive feedback on their relational impact and experiment with new behaviours.\n9. Group Cohesiveness: The group analogue of the therapeutic alliance; mutual belonging and warmth.\n10. Catharsis: Expressing suppressed emotions within a validating social matrix.\n11. Existential Factors: Accepting personal responsibility for authoring one\'s life.\n\nMechanically, groups function optimally with 7 to 8 members meeting weekly for 90 minutes. Group life progresses through Bruce Tuckman\'s developmental stages: Forming (orientation/politeness), Storming (conflict and testing boundaries), Norming (cohesion and mutual trust), Performing (working through deep interpersonal themes), and Adjourning (termination and consolidation). Empirical meta-analyses confirm that group therapy is equally efficacious to individual therapy, offering unique interpersonal benefits.',
