        modelAnswer: 'Couples and Relationship Counselling is a specialized clinical discipline designed to help romantic partners resolve destructive communication cycles, heal attachment injuries, and foster enduring intimacy. Modern relationship science is primarily defined by the empirical discoveries of John Gottman and the attachment framework of Sue Johnson.\n\nJohn M. Gottman (*The Seven Principles for Making Marriage Work*, 1999) conducted longitudinal observational research tracking couples\' verbal and physiological interactions during conflict. Gottman discovered that relational stability is dictated by how couples manage conflict, identifying the "Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse"—four communication patterns that predict relationship dissolution with over 90% accuracy:\n1. Criticism: Attacking the partner\'s personality rather than a specific behaviour. Antidote: Gentle Start-Up using "I" statements.\n2. Contempt: Sarcasm, mockery, eye-rolling, and disgust, conveying moral superiority. Contempt is the single most lethal predictor of divorce. Antidote: Creating a culture of appreciation.\n3. Defensiveness: Counter-attacking or playing the innocent victim. Antidote: Taking responsibility for one\'s part.\n4. Stonewalling: Emotionally and physically withdrawing, typically occurring when physiological flooding (heart rate > 100 bpm) overwhelms executive functioning. Antidote: Taking a 20-minute physiological timeout to self-soothe.\n\nGottman demonstrated that stable couples maintain a "Magic Ratio" of at least 5 positive interactions to every 1 negative interaction during conflict and utilize "Repair Attempts" to de-escalate tension.\n\nComplementing Gottman, Dr. Sue Johnson (*The Practice of Emotionally Focused Couple Therapy*, 2004) developed Emotion-Focused Therapy (EFT). Grounded in adult attachment theory, EFT views marital distress as an attachment panic. Couples become trapped in negative cycles (most commonly the Pursue-Withdraw dance: one partner criticizes to protest emotional disconnection, prompting the other to retreat into avoidant silence). EFT helps partners access and communicate their primary vulnerable attachment emotions (fear of unworthiness, terror of abandonment), establishing a secure emotional bond.\n\nCrucially, couples therapy is strictly contraindicated in the presence of ongoing Intimate Partner Violence (IPV), active untreated addiction, or ongoing unacknowledged affairs, where joint sessions endanger victim safety.',
